1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:08,235
Hello and welcome to the Owl's Lessons! Last time, you learned how to improve against the computer.

2
00:00:07,935 --> 00:00:12,385
Today, things get serious: online games against real players.

3
00:00:13,914 --> 00:00:18,455
And with them comes a number everyone watches: the ELO rating.

4
00:00:18,155 --> 00:00:22,285
How it's calculated, and how to make it climb. Let's go!

5
00:00:24,185 --> 00:00:28,186
Playing online on TickGammon is simple and free.

6
00:00:27,886 --> 00:00:32,890
Everything happens in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

7
00:00:33,313 --> 00:00:38,017
Three steps are enough. First, create your free account, in one minute.

8
00:00:37,717 --> 00:00:43,475
Next, choose your game: an online player, a friend or a bot. And finally, roll the dice!

9
00:00:46,883 --> 00:00:49,144
Your account is the same everywhere.

10
00:00:48,844 --> 00:00:53,120
You can start a game on your computer and carry on with it on your phone.

11
00:00:55,478 --> 00:01:01,272
Before sending a challenge, you choose the match length, that is, the number of points needed to win.

12
00:01:02,951 --> 00:01:09,743
You also choose the pace. In Zen, you take all the time you want. In Classic, the clock is comfortable.

13
00:01:10,329 --> 00:01:15,183
In Blitz, you have to play fast. And in Bullet, every second counts!

14
00:01:14,883 --> 00:01:19,402
Start with a relaxed pace, then speed up once you feel at ease.

15
00:01:19,552 --> 00:01:21,788
You can also play with your friends.

16
00:01:21,488 --> 00:01:26,091
Add them, and challenge them even when they're offline: they get a notification.

17
00:01:25,791 --> 00:01:28,565
You can even watch their games as a spectator.

18
00:01:32,377 --> 00:01:34,578
Now let's talk about the ELO rating.

19
00:01:34,278 --> 00:01:38,802
It's a number that measures your playing level, compared with the other players.

20
00:01:39,343 --> 00:01:44,312
The system comes from chess. Many games use it, because it's simple and fair.

21
00:01:45,247 --> 00:01:48,664
On TickGammon, everyone starts at fifteen hundred.

22
00:01:48,364 --> 00:01:53,465
After that, every game against a real player makes your rating go up or down.

23
00:01:54,119 --> 00:01:58,516
And the higher your rating, the higher you climb in the rankings.

24
00:01:58,216 --> 00:02:01,983
Only real players count: no bots, and no team accounts.

25
00:02:04,981 --> 00:02:09,041
So how does your rating move? It all depends on your opponent.

26
00:02:09,191 --> 00:02:14,108
If you beat a player at your level, you gain points, and they lose the same amount.

27
00:02:13,808 --> 00:02:17,501
With two new players at fifteen hundred, it's sixteen points.

28
00:02:18,592 --> 00:02:21,388
If you beat a stronger player, you gain more.

29
00:02:21,088 --> 00:02:26,268
Against an opponent rated two hundred points higher, a win brings you about twenty-four.

30
00:02:27,164 --> 00:02:32,033
On the other hand, beating a much weaker player brings you almost nothing.

31
00:02:31,733 --> 00:02:33,947
And losing to them costs a lot!

32
00:02:36,084 --> 00:02:40,480
Your rating also moves faster or slower depending on your experience.

33
00:02:40,743 --> 00:02:45,390
During your first fifty games, it moves a lot: the system is getting to know you.

34
00:02:46,842 --> 00:02:49,470
After that, it gradually settles down.

35
00:02:49,170 --> 00:02:53,636
After three hundred games, each match only moves it by a few points.

36
00:02:54,585 --> 00:02:59,695
So don't panic if you lose a few points at the start. It's normal, and you'll soon win them back.

37
00:03:02,376 --> 00:03:05,492
The rankings are the showcase of your progress.

38
00:03:05,192 --> 00:03:09,566
There you can see your rank, your rating, your wins and your losses.

39
00:03:10,913 --> 00:03:16,086
Some rating milestones also unlock achievements, with rewards to show off on your profile.

40
00:03:16,588 --> 00:03:20,213
And remember: on TickGammon, it's all just a game.

41
00:03:19,913 --> 00:03:26,465
TGC are an in-game currency, offered every twelve hours. They can't be withdrawn or converted.

42
00:03:26,615 --> 00:03:31,483
What really matters is your level. And the ELO rating is there to measure it.

43
00:03:33,383 --> 00:03:36,285
Here are four tips to make your rating climb.

44
00:03:37,053 --> 00:03:43,974
First tip: play regularly. A few games every day are better than one long session once a month.

45
00:03:45,156 --> 00:03:50,503
Second tip: after a loss, don't jump straight back in out of frustration.

46
00:03:50,203 --> 00:03:52,992
Take a break, then analyse the game.

47
00:03:53,875 --> 00:04:00,207
Third tip: choose opponents at your level, or slightly stronger. That's where you learn the most.

48
00:04:01,116 --> 00:04:05,394
And fourth tip: use everything you've seen in these lessons.

49
00:04:05,094 --> 00:04:10,235
The pip count, the doubling cube, the probabilities… every detail counts.

50
00:04:12,831 --> 00:04:15,361
A word about the doubling cube online.

51
00:04:15,061 --> 00:04:19,409
In a match, it changes everything, and it also weighs on your rating.

52
00:04:19,559 --> 00:04:24,131
A gammon, or a game with the cube at a high value, can decide the match.

53
00:04:23,831 --> 00:04:27,810
Think of the twenty-five percent rule before you take or pass.

54
00:04:28,561 --> 00:04:34,976
And after every match, open the analysis. Your cube decisions are rated there, just like your moves.

55
00:04:37,730 --> 00:04:47,857
And there you go, you now know how to play online: match lengths, paces, your friends, and above all the ELO rating, which climbs when you beat stronger players!

56
00:04:48,007 --> 00:04:55,372
To get started, play for free against other players or against the computer, in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

57
00:04:55,072 --> 00:05:00,655
Next time, I'll introduce tournaments, clubs and lessons. Subscribe so you don't miss it!

58
00:05:00,355 --> 00:05:02,436
See you soon around the board!

